{
  "schema": "nl-search-filter-schema.md",
  "catalogCommit": "bedf0a63f27299dd9210b8ccb6f027bca310c08f",
  "note": "These cases test parse output: a natural-language question mapped to an NLSearchFilter. Because a model is non-deterministic, compare loosely: every key in `required` must be present and equal; keys in `optional` may appear with that value but are not required; a non-null field that is not in `required` or `optional` fails; every name in `mustBeNull` must be null or absent (the model must not invent it). If `providerAcceptable` is set, `required.provider` may be any of those strings. If `titleFolded` is set, compare the folded title rather than the raw string. `outcome` is `apply` (run apply() on the validated filter), `empty-filter` (all-null; use keyword CatalogSearch and do not call apply), or `validate` (an unknown genre, category, kind, watched value, or provider must be nulled or the parse rejected so it cannot reach apply). `switchesTo` is `movies`, `episodes`, or `stay`, derived from `kind`: movie or episode selects that list (switch if the other list is showing; stay if already there); any or a missing kind stays on the current list. The deterministic filter-to-results step is not covered here and must be unit-tested exactly against apply(). Cases with a `parse` object (source `validation-rules`, added 17 Nov 2026) test validation, not the model: feed `parse` to the validator as the model output, then compare the validated filter to `required` / `mustBeNull` with the same loose rule (so a field that should be dropped must be null).",
